#
# Line endings are load-bearing here. The committed generated files are compared BYTE-FOR-BYTE by
# the reproducibility gates: `make verify-offline` diffs the recompute against data/derived, and the
# `ledger index` audit diffs the regenerated INDEX.md against the committed one. Their bytes must match
# exactly what the generating code writes on every platform — Python's csv module writes CRLF; json.dump
# and the digest write LF. Without this, a clone on a system with core.autocrlf (Windows default) would
# check these out with different bytes than the deterministic recompute produces, and the gate would
# fail spuriously — breaking the project's core "byte-identical offline" guarantee.
#
# The CSVs carry CRLF: mark them binary so git preserves their exact bytes with NO conversion anywhere
# (store-exact, checkout-exact) — the simplest guarantee for a byte-identical-reproduction repo, and it
# leaves the already-committed blobs untouched. The LF files stay diffable text but are pinned to LF so
# autocrlf can't add carriage returns on checkout.
data/**/*.csv    -text
data/**/*.json   text eol=lf
ledger/INDEX.md  text eol=lf

# Everything else keeps git's default handling (no forced conversion); source and prose stay LF.
